Z||000000||MR2KLR||11-24-2001||05:05 PM||||<BR>I'm geting my first non-84 fiero tomorow and it needs a fule pump. I don't have access to a lift. I have 4 jackstands and a floor jack. I know I need to drop the tank. How high does the car need to be to do this. any special tools needed to do it?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://members.tripod.com/wawoonigootphet/images/af/3in1_vu1.jpg">  <A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jay||11-24-2001||05:13 PM||||That's all you need!  The higher you get it, the easier it is.  You'll need to undo the straps that hold the tank in.  If it's rusted then spray them down with WD40 or liquid wrench.  If I remember correctly, you only have to undo one side of the strap and then bend it over so the tank will clear.  Not that difficult but easier if you have two people.  Shouldn't take that long.  Is the tank full or empty?<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig2_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MR2KLR||11-24-2001||05:17 PM||||I asume the tank need to come out from under the car to swap pumps. But, How tall/Deep is the tank How high does it NEED to be I don't have the best jackstands. I don't want to get the car up in the air and then find out that I need to get taller stands.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||11-24-2001||05:30 PM||||<P>I have changed several fuel pumps with just  a floor jack and some jack stands.<P>I have done it with only 2 jackstands, but 4 is MUCH better.  You will need to raise the car about 18" (+/-) to get the tank out.  <P>You don't need any special tools, just a ratchet, 13mm socket, 7mm socket and some flat blade screwdrivers.<P>With the car in the air remove the center brace (held on by four 13mm bolts).  Now remove the four small brackets that hold the heater /AC lines that run next to the tank and carefully pull them away from the tank (just an inch or so).  Now remove the big metal heatshield from the firewall (held on with four 7mm screws.  This will give you better access to the hoses that need to come off.<P>Take a flat blade screwdriver and loosen the clamps on the two main hoses attached to the tank (one is about 1/2", the other about 2" in diameter).  There are some smaller lines that need to be disconnected as well......two of them are easy to see, they are next to the main hoses.  One has a single clamp and the other has two.....these are the "to & from" lines.  There is a third line that needs to be removed as well.....you will see it run past the other two lines and heads towards the passenger side of the engine compartment.  A stubby screwdriver works well to get this one off.<P>Now you need to unplug the fuel pump harness......it is a yellow plug that is in the same proximity as the third line (by the passenger side decklid hinge).  You can get to it from below or from the top.....take your pick.<P>Now carefully pry the rubber hoses off of the solid lines.......the main hoses might be on there pretty good, but they'll come off.  If they are tough you can wait until you remove the tank brackets to persuade it a little bit (use the weight of the tank to your advantage).<P>Now take a 13mm socket (a 3" extension might help) and remove the two clamps that hold the tank in place.  Once they are loose and out of the way you can slowly start lowering the tank.  I am usually directly underneath the tank at this point, so rest the tank on your chest and make sure that everything is loose.....the third line and the wiring must be fished through a small hold in the firewall, so make sure these are freed up before lowering the rest of the way.  <P>With the tank on the ground just slide it from underneath the car and remove the cam on the top of the tank and pull out the sending unit.  Replace the pump & strainer and reinstall!<P>It really isn't a tough job.....for the first time out plan on 3-5 hours from start to finish.  I have gotten it down to about an hour or so, but I've had practice (too much practice, if you ask me).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-25-2001||12:53 AM||||I generally don't recomend putting a car on 4 jack stands. If you do, make sure you have a very flat work area, preferably on concrete. It can be very easy to destabilize 4 stands, especially cheap ones. make certain the car is resting solidly on all 4 stands.<P>For something that needs allot of room, like the fuel tank or inline transmission, a set of ramps works better. For a Fiero tank it would probably be better to back onto the ramps.<P>Ramps have a larger footprint but even they usually prefere to be on concrete. If you are working on dirt or blacktop then you really need some metal plates or 1/2inch+ plywood to prevent the stands/ramps from sinking. those thin metal edges on most ramps and cheap jack stands sink allot easier than people think.<P>Never ever use concrete/cinder blocks. They have a nasty habit of shattering sudenly. And yes, I do know poeple who have been injured or killed doing that.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroRacer||11-26-2001||12:12 AM||||No No No No No No No, bad.  Let me tell you how it's done.<P>You go to Kragens, tell them you need a universal inline fuel pump to feed your engine (2.5 or 2.8), then you just installed it right next to the gas tank.  Snip the stock wires, wire the positive and negative to the fuel pump.  Get in car, put key in, start up, check for leaks, get back in car, the burn rice rockets all day!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||johnt671||11-26-2001||08:13 AM||||Wow. That inline fuel pump sounds like a winner to me.  Will it still pump ok with the old pump in?  A few more details please.||||reg||1
